\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Layer Cake occupied a quarter of a floor halfway up Malabar Tower, a frosted, many-tiered bonbon of a building with icing-thick white fa\'e7ades and balustrades so scrolled they seemed designed for cherubs to slide down, if only cherubs could afford the valet hover-pad levy. Its tagline, \'93Let them eat Layer,\'94 was etched in tastefully understated calligraphy over its revolving, self-polishing doors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Inside, the light arrived by a million routes: bouncing through prisms, diffused through mother-of-pearl chandeliers, and spectrally moistened until it trickled down over the patrons like liquid noon. The intent, naturally, was celestial radiance. The result: dazzled customers blinking into their gold-leaf cappuccinos, some shading their eyes behind designer bifocals, others poking at plated desserts with the nervousness of a rookie bomb disposal wallaby.\
The clientele sat at blue-glass tables shaped like stacked concentric halos\'97every inch engineered to communicate both elevation and exclusion. Waiters glided about, modishly underfed and wearing uniforms that seemed borrowed from a minimalist fencing club.\
No menu at Layer Cake could be read directly; it was displayed on holo-projected slices that spiralled like lethargic jellyfish and dissolved if your credit didn\'92t clear.\
The famous house coffee beans, whose renowned qualities have been noted variously as: \'93collective memory of every accountant\'92s lunchroom\'94; \'93sharp, like a punch in the kidneys with a wad of rolled-up term sheets\'94; \'93a lingering suspicion of spoiled fruit\'94; \'93tannins reminiscent of old video tape soaked in used gearbox oil\'94; \'93a pernicious finish of latter day capitalism with extra foam\'94; and, \'93pairs beautifully with discount croissants or barcoded apocalypse rations\'94. None of which was surprising, since they were allegedly passed through the digestive tracts of up-and-coming fashion models in a process the investors called \'93dietary philanthropy\'94. But they were criminally expensive, which is exactly why the Airborne craved to be seen attempting to drink them.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Johnny Stoat sat, sulking over a gold-leaf cappuccino that cost more than a part-time model-waiter made in a month, ensconced at the semi-mythical VIP Alcove of Layer Cake. Anyone with even a passing acquaintance with caf\'e9 topography knew this table\'92s main draw was not the view\'97unless you found decorative gas meters arousing\'97but the unspoken power implied by its strategic elevation over the rest of the floor. The kind of spot reserved for minor media personalities, hedge fund larvae, and regulars who could fake an accent when booking. The VIP Alcove was reserved for the indiscriminating status-seeker.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The staff, who understood both the physics and metaphysics of Layer Cake\'92s table hierarchy, had sized Johnny up on entry. They overruled his initial trajectory toward the actual VIP zone by the windows with something about \'93dress code for elbows\'94. Johnny settled for the Alcove with all the tactical fervour of a NATO operation, having calculated that from here he could both monitor Hayley\'92s entrance and ensure she would be forced to sit under the blast of the air conditioner. The staff condescended to let him roost there, provided he didn\'92t try to order anything containing genuine truffle.\
Hayley arrived, slid into the seat opposite from him, and immediately drew her cardigan tighter around her shoulders.\
\'93Can I get you anything?\'94 Johnny offered.\
Hayley eyed Johnny\'92s cappuccino. \'93I\'92ll have what you\'92re having. And a glass of hot water.\'94\
Johnny eventually attracted the attention of a waitress and ordered. \'93Another gold-leaf cappuccino, please.\'94\
That done, Hayley went straight to business. \'93You say you know where Sarah Jacobson is.\'94\
\'93I do.\'94 Smug.\
\'93Where?\'94\
\'93I can\'92t say. Precisely.\'94 Even smugger.\
Hayley\'92s coffee arrived. Johnny watched as she retrieved a tissue from her bag and carefully smoothed it flat on the table. Then, with delicate precision, she lifted each fleck of gold leaf from her coffee and arranged it on the tissue. Once she had gathered all the glittering fragments, she folded up the tissue and tucked it neatly back into her bag.\
\'93Are you going to drink that?\'94 Johnny asked, becoming the Inquisitor.\
Hayley pushed the cup and saucer away. \'93No chance. The coffee here tastes like rabbit shit.\'94\
Johnny was offended. No one was meant to call out the truth of the coffee here.\
\'93Alright,\'94 Hayley said. \'93What can you tell me?\'94\
\'93She was with me at the Memphis Mafia Bar the night before Chantara was captured for terrorism. He was there. He engineered her kidnapping.\'94\
\'93Chantara kidnapped Sarah Jacobson?\'94\
\'93Right before my eyes. I tried to stop him. The PAPER agents did nothing.\'94\
Hayley let him ramble: draw out inconsistencies, spot the truth. \'93You, off the tourist trail in the Ghetto. That I find hard to believe.\'94\
Stoat puffed, believably aggrieved. \'93Believe me, detective, it wasn\'92t my choice. She dragged me. She\'92s always into those places.\'94\
\'93Name one other.\'94\
\'93The Blue Shade.\'94 He won that round. \'93I can\'92t remember the rest. There were too many.\'94\
Hayley saw through him\'97maybe two Elvi bars, max. She let an eyebrow arch.\
\'93Sarah, she gets obsessed, it\'92s like watching a sport to her\'97Elvi karaoke. You know the tunes are good\'85 but the lyrics? \'91A Little Less Condensation\'92\'97what the tax is that? They always fight over the words. Sarah loved it.\'94\
\'93You love her, don\'92t you?\'94\
\'93What? Yes!\'94 Johnny gulped. \'93I think\'85 but what is love, really?\'94\
\'93You want her, then.\'94\
\'93Who wouldn\'92t? There are\'97well, there are many viable options out there. But Sarah\'85\'94\
Hayley watched yet another man caught between weakness and the fear of showing it.\
\'93Where is she now?\'94\
\'93The Elvi have her under house arrest in the Ghetto. And Chantara is there pulling all the strings.\'94\
\'93You\'92ve seen them there?\'94\
\'93Yes,\'94 Johnny blurted before he realised what he was admitting.  \
\'93How long ago?\'94\
\'93Five weeks after...\'94  \
\'93Your date at the Memphis Mafia Bar?\'94\
Johnny smiled when Hayley affirmed it was a date. \'93Yes,\'94 he said, more pleased with himself than he should be.  \
A waiter glided past. Hayley let the silence hold.\
\'93Is she still there?\'94\
\'93I cannot say.\'94\
\'93You cannot say, or you don\'92t know?\'94\
\'93I surmise she is still there.\'94\
\'93Where?\'94\
\'93I will not say, specifically, detective, but I want you to understand: please, please, find her.\'94\
\'93Then tell me.\'94\
\'93It is more than my life is possibly worth.\'94\
Hayley scoffed for effect. \'93You\'92re afraid of the Elvi\'97conditioned to entertain and perform hospitality tasks\'97and a cleaner?\'94\
\'93You should have seen the way he was looking at me.\'94\
\'93Go on.\'94\
\'93At the Memphis Mafia Bar, I remember him sitting at a table against the wall, throwing down beer after beer, just staring at us. I thought he was a bitter nobody at the time. Looking back on it now, knowing who he is and what he\'92s done, I realise... the way he was staring at me, he was in a jealous rage, furious, even through his burns and his frizzled-away hair.\'94\
\'93You don\'92t think he was perhaps just in pain because of his injuries?\'94\
\'93Well, he deserved all he got after bombing... that place... down there in...\'92 Johnny waved his hand vaguely. \
\'93I\'92m surprised you\'92d have any concern over an incident in a Cloudborne bar.\'94  \
\'93I am a man of certain compassions, detective.\'94\
Hayley doubted it. \'93So you were there, on a date... it was all going swimmingly, presumably.\'94\
Johnny smiled. \'93Indeed.\'94\
\'93Until what?\'94\
\'93Well, the most outrageous thing which, since then, I wonder if it might be connected with Sarah\'92s kidnapping.\'94\
\'93Which was?\'94\
\'93This sounds preposterous, but I assure you it really happened.\'94\
\'93Go on.\'94\
\'93Three wizened old men came up to our table\'97Sarah\'92s and mine. They were wearing one-piece pyjama outfits and looked like their sleep had been interrupted. They were clearly out of their locomotives, detective.\'94\
Hayley blinked. She was expecting the PAPER agents to enter the story here\'97that would have been the proper, rehearsed thing to say. But Hayley found herself believing this part of the story, so she waited for more. Johnny appeared unable to spit out the next piece, so Hayley prompted, \'93What happened?\'94\
Johnny looked as if he was trying to cough up\'97or gag down\'97a bad prawn. \'93They asked Sarah if they could get her pregnant.\'94\
Hayley sputtered an abrupt laugh, then caught it. 
\f2\i Tax, that must be true,
\f1\i0  she thought.  \
\'93I know, right? But the more I think about it, the more I think unnatural forces are at play, detective. Perhaps there\'92s some, I dunno, evil at work, ensnaring Sarah.\'94\
\'93What makes you say that?\'94\
Johnny blanched. He was well off script now. \'93The old geezers were from the Travelator,\'94 he said, as if that would explain anything\'97everything\'97if that could explain everything.  \
\'93I see.\'94\
\'93And that\'92s when the PAPER agents busted in. One of them shot a chandelier and, while everyone was in shock, they locked the place down. Military precision, detective. Military precision.\'94\
\'93You must have been terrified,\'94 Hayley said.  \
Johnny straightened and tried his best imperious demeanour. \'93I was clarified, detective. Crystallised. I am good in a crisis.\'94\
In Hayley\'92s experience, anyone who claimed they were good in a crisis was always gibbering jelly in a crisis. She might as well let Johnny have his moment now. You never know what you might learn.  \
\'93The leader of the PAPER squad,\'94 Johnny said sombrely, nodding and summoning a reserve of courage Hayley doubted he had, \'93he trained his pistol on Sarah, and demanded we hand her over.\'94\
\'93Hand Sarah over? Are you sure?\'94\
\'93Quite certain, detective. And I had no doubt he would get violent, unless I did something. I mean, I was the only one in the room with the courage, the breeding, you see... I was the only Airborne there.\'94\
\'93Sarah Jacobson is Airborne, isn\'92t she?\'94\
\'93The only Airborne 
\f2\i man
\f1\i0 , detective.\'94\
\'93Yes, of course. Carry on.\'94\
\'93It was my duty to carry the day, detective. So, at possible risk of my life\'97although I\'92m quite confident of my conflict resolution skills\'97\'94 Johnny paused to see if Hayley was buying it. \'93\'97I calmly put myself between Sarah and the agent. \'91I will have her!\'92 the agent said.\'94 Johnny was now back on-piste as he groomed his story. \'93So I said, dear, young agent\'97\'94 Johnny hesitated; even he knew that was bull tax. \'93Agent, I wonder if you could consider for a moment the enormity of the storm you find yourself in. Then: \'91I will have her death,\'92 the agent snarled. Then the strangest words came out of my mouth, detective, like I could see into the agent\'92s soul. Sometimes I know the exact right thing to say.\'94\
Hayley doubted Johnny ever had the right thing to say.  \
\'93I said to him, \'91You don\'92t want to kill her. You want to please her. You want to worship her.\'92\'94\
Hayley knew who wanted to please and worship Sarah.  \
\'93I had him, detective. I cut him to the quick. He was completely neutralised.\'94\
\'93Astounding, Mr Stoat. But presumably the Elvi intervened?\'94\
\'93Intervened? They stole that moment from me\'97as the agent confronted and accepted his fallibility\'97to start one of their interminable bar fights.\'94\
